prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a strategy mini-game featuring patrolling soldiers and landmines. Soldiers patrol along paths on a grid map. The player clicks the ground to place landmines; when a soldier approaches a landmine, it triggers an explosion effect and the soldier is eliminated.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import through ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { GLTFLoader } from 'three/addons/loaders/GLTFLoader.js';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Model Loading]
(1) Use GLTFLoader to load assets/RobotExpressive.glb. This model serves as a soldier character and contains animation clips: Walk, Idle, and possibly Run / Death, etc. Iterate through gltf.animations to find clips whose names contain these keywords;
(2) Use SkeletonUtils.clone to create 3 cloned soldiers, each with an independent AnimationMixer;
(3) After loading is complete, scale the soldiers to an appropriate size (scale approximately 0.5-1.0) and place each one at the starting point of its patrol route;

[Grid Map]
(4) Create a 12×12 grid map (each cell is 1×1 units), represented by a light gray plane and dark gray grid lines;
(5) The grid extends from (0,0,0) to (12,0,12), and the center coordinates of each cell are (col+0.5, 0, row+0.5);
(6) Use OrbitControls to allow the player to rotate and zoom the view of the map;

[Patrol System]
(7) Each of the 3 soldiers has a predefined patrol route (waypoint array), for example:
  - Soldier 1: [(1,0,1), (1,0,6), (6,0,6), (6,0,1)] loop
  - Soldier 2: [(10,0,2), (10,0,10), (3,0,10)] loop
  - Soldier 3: [(5,0,5), (5,0,11), (11,0,11), (11,0,5)] loop
(8) Soldiers move along the waypoints at a speed of 2 m/s. After reaching one waypoint, they automatically turn toward the next one; after reaching the final waypoint, they return to the first one (looping patrol);
(9) Play the Walk animation while moving. When briefly pausing for 0.5 seconds upon reaching a waypoint, play the Idle animation;
(10) Soldiers face the direction of movement (turn automatically);

[Landmine System]
(11) The player clicks a map cell to place a landmine (use Raycaster to detect the ground grid). Represent a landmine as a small red sphere (radius 0.2), positioned at the center of the cell with y=0.2;
(12) Each cell can contain at most one landmine. A cell that already contains a landmine cannot receive another one;
(13) At most 10 landmines may exist simultaneously (when this limit is exceeded, display "Landmines full");

[Explosion Detection]
(14) On every frame, check the distance between every surviving soldier and every landmine. Trigger an explosion when the distance is < 1.5 units;
(15) Explosion effect: generate a group of particles (30-50 small spheres) at the landmine's position. They spread outward and disappear within 1 second (shrink + fade out). The landmine also disappears;
(16) For a soldier caught in the explosion: play the Death animation if the model has one; otherwise, make the soldier fall over (rotate 90 degrees around the x-axis). Remove the soldier from the scene after 1.5 seconds;

[HUD and State Exposure]
(17) Display the following in the upper-left HUD:
  - Number of surviving soldiers (id="soldiersAlive")
  - Number of placed landmines (id="minesPlaced")
  - Number of explosions (id="explosions")
  - Game state (id="gameState", displaying playing / victory)
(18) When all 3 soldiers have been eliminated, display "Victory!" and change gameState to victory;

[State Exposure (Required)]
Update window.__3D_STATE__ on every frame:
{
  modelLoaded: boolean,        // Whether the soldier model has finished loading
  soldiersAlive: number,       // Number of surviving soldiers
  minesPlaced: number,         // Number of landmines currently on the field
  explosions: number,          // Cumulative number of explosions
  gameState: string,           // 'playing' | 'victory'
  soldierPositions: {x:number, z:number}[],  // Positions of all surviving soldiers
  minePositions: {x:number, z:number}[]      // Positions of all landmines
}

Output a single HTML file, place it in the same directory as the assets/ folder, and run it through a local HTTP server.
